    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "cde1cd05_20f05a6f",
        "filename": "framework/java/android/bluetooth/BluetoothSocketSettings.java",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 46,
      "author": {
        "id": 1881060
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-21T17:41:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t understand the usage of these\n\nYou are using them to configure encryption and authentication, but the security level is not used as one entity. The usage in the other file split it to `auth` and to `encrypt` before calling the BluetoothSocket manager.\n\nShould we simply have two boolean variable in BluetoothSocketSettings to represent encryption and authentication, and let the apps sets them as they want independently ?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 39,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 46,
        "endChar": 80
      },
      "revId": "8525459eb570c69a5df1db9227d90760cc213fb4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ff427a29_91690ca2",
        "filename": "framework/java/android/bluetooth/BluetoothSocketSettings.java",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 46,
      "author": {
        "id": 3469321
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-21T22:24:22Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "auth and encrypt used in internal functions today.\nCurrently there are two variants public apis, secure vs insecure - where secure sets both to true and insecure sets both flags to false.\nWe wanted to align with similar conventions at the SDK and introduce another level which allows \"encryption only\" sockets. Exposing those two flags directly might be too much information for the app developers and end up having more number of setters while building the SocketSettings data structure.\n\nI did have a short sync with Jack on last Friday and he was fine with this structure in general. please let me know if you have more concerns on this approach.",
      "parentUuid": "cde1cd05_20f05a6f",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 39,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 46,
        "endChar": 80
      },
      "revId": "8525459eb570c69a5df1db9227d90760cc213fb4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "583cb9dd_e443b1e8",
        "filename": "framework/java/android/bluetooth/BluetoothSocketSettings.java",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 46,
      "author": {
        "id": 1881060
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-21T23:21:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think the current implementation is more confusing, and the are name hard to read.\nBut that may be because I am not used to this code.\n\n\u003e Exposing those two flags directly might be too much information for the app developers\n\nThe two flag are still exposed with the current implementation as the enum is a joint mix of the two flags.\n\n\u003e more number of setters while building the SocketSettings data structure.\n\nI don\u0027t consider this to be an issue. It easier to have multiple simple purpose setter, than one setter that does hidden work.\n\n----\n\nIn the end, I don\u0027t own this code. You should be able to do as you think is best here.",
      "parentUuid": "ff427a29_91690ca2",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 39,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 46,
        "endChar": 80
      },
      "revId": "8525459eb570c69a5df1db9227d90760cc213fb4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
